"21-34","label":"{213̅4}","index":[2,1,-3,4],"name":"v, scalenohedron (dogtooth)","color":"#e6b77a","normals":[[0.8824053,0.3056742,0.3576649],[-0.7059242,0.6113483,0.3576649],[-0.1764811,0.9170225,0.3576649],[-0.8824053,-0.3056742,-0.3576649],[-0.1764811,-0.9170225,0.3576649],[0.8824053,-0.3056742,0.3576649],[0.7059242,-0.6113483,-0.3576649],[-0.7059242,-0.6113483,0.3576649],[0.1764811,-0.9170225,-0.3576649],[0.1764811,0.9170225,-0.3576649],[-0.8824053,0.3056742,-0.3576649],[0.7059242,0.6113483,-0.3576649]],"hkls":[[2,1,4],[-3,2,4],[-2,3,4],[-2,-1,-4],[1,-3,4],[3,-1,4],[3,-2,-4],[-1,-2,4],[2,-3,-4],[-1,3,-4],[-3,1,-4],[1,2,-4]]}],"basis":[[4.322333,-2.4955,0.0],[0.0,4.991,0.0],[0.0,0.0,17.062]],"reciprocal":[[0.23135655,0.0,0.0],[0.11567827,0.20036065,-0.0],[-0.0,-0.0,0.05860978]],"axis":[0.0,0.0,1.0],"measure_axis":[0.0,0.0,1.0],"pg_order":12,"matrix":{"title":"Prism length × termination","subtitle":"Calcite's famous variety comes from a few forms: the cleavage rhombohedron r, the flat e (nailhead spar), the steep M, the scalenohedron v (dogtooth spar), the prism m and the pinacoid c. Columns run roughly from low to high supersaturation, and rows from tablets to fibres.","row_title":"Prism development","col_title":"Termination","rows":[{"label":"T","detail":"tabular"},{"label":"Q","detail":"no prism"},{"label":"P1","detail":"short prism"},{"label":"P2","detail":"prismatic"},{"label":"P3","detail":"acicular"}],"cols":[{"label":"e","detail":"flat rhombohedron"},{"label":"r","detail":"cleavage rhombohedron"},{"label":"M","detail":"steep rhombohedron"},{"label":"v","detail":"scalenohedron"}],"note":"Dana's morphological cell has c one quarter of the structural c, so his cleavage rhombohedron (101̄1) is {101̄4} here and the scalenohedron v(213̄1) is {213̄4}. r∧r′ = 74.93° against Dana's 74°55′. The ordering of terminations by supersaturation follows the speleothem study of Gonzalez et al. (1992) and is qualitative."},"about":"\n<p>Calcite has more recorded crystal forms than almost any other mineral, but a handful explain most habits. In\nspeleothems, below about 6× supersaturation only rhombohedra grow; scalenohedra, steep rhombohedra, prism and\npinacoid appear at higher supersaturation (Gonzalez et al. 1992). Mg²⁺ adsorbed on the {101̄4} steps changes growth\n(Davis et al. 2000) and favours fibrous, c-elongated marine calcite (Folk 1974), while reactant supply rate also\nmatters (Given &amp; Wilkinson 1985). Trace elements are incorporated step by step on the growth hillocks (Paquette\n&amp; Reeder 1995). These are the same crystals now dated by LA-ICP-MS U–Pb (Roberts et al. 2020) and used for clumped-isotope\nthermometry.</p>","habits":[{"name":"Cleavage rhombohedron","text":"r{101̄4} alone (row Q, column r)."},{"name":"Nailhead spar","text":"Short prism capped by the flat rhombohedron e (row P1, column e)."},{"name":"Dogtooth spar","text":"The scalenohedron v (column v)."},{"name":"Fibrous / columnar","text":"Long m prisms (rows P2–P3)."},{"name":"Paper spar","text":"Thin plates on c{0001} (row T)."}],"references":[{"citation":"Maslen, E.N., Streltsov, V.A., Streltsova, N.R. (1993). X-ray study of the electron density in calcite, CaCO<sub>3</sub>. <i>Acta Crystallographica B</i> 49, 636–641.","doi":"10.1107/S0108768193002575","url":null,"note":"The cell used here.","tags":["lattice"]},{"citation":"Gonzalez, L.A., Carpenter, S.J., Lohmann, K.C. (1992). Inorganic calcite morphology: roles of fluid chemistry and fluid flow. <i>Journal of Sedimentary Petrology</i> 62, 382–399.","doi":"10.1306/D426790B-2B26-11D7-8648000102C1865D","url":null,"note":"Speleothem calcite habit versus supersaturation.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Davis, K.J., Dove, P.M., De Yoreo, J.J. (2000). The role of Mg<sup>2+</sup> as an impurity in calcite growth. <i>Science</i> 290, 1134–1137.","doi":"10.1126/science.290.5494.1134","url":null,"note":"AFM study of Mg effects on {101̄4} growth.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Paquette, J., Reeder, R.J. (1995). Relationship between surface structure, growth mechanism, and trace element incorporation in calcite. <i>Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta</i> 59, 735–749.","doi":"10.1016/0016-7037(95)00004-J","url":null,"note":"Step-specific trace-element uptake on {101̄4} growth hillocks.","tags":["geochemistry"]},{"citation":"Roberts, N.M.W., Drost, K., Horstwood, M.S.A., Condon, D.J., Chew, D., Drake, H., et al. (2020). Laser ablation inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (LA-ICP-MS) U–Pb carbonate geochronology: strategies, progress, and limitations. <i>Geochronology</i> 2, 33–61.","doi":"10.5194/gchron-2-33-2020","url":null,"note":"Review of calcite U–Pb dating.","tags":["geochronology"]},{"citation":"Ghosh, P., Adkins, J., Affek, H., Balta, B., Guo, W., Schauble, E.A., Schrag, D., Eiler, J.M. (2006). <sup>13</sup>C–<sup>18</sup>O bonds in carbonate minerals: a new kind of paleothermometer. <i>Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta</i> 70, 1439–1456.","doi":"10.1016/j.gca.2005.11.014","url":null,"note":"Clumped-isotope thermometry.","tags":["thermometry"]},{"citation":"Folk, R.L. (1974). The natural history of crystalline calcium carbonate; effect of magnesium content and salinity. <i>Journal of Sedimentary Petrology</i> 44, 40–53.","doi":"10.1306/74D72973-2B21-11D7-8648000102C1865D","url":null,"note":"Classic model: Mg poisoning gives fibrous calcite elongated along c, versus equant meteoric calcite.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Given, R.K., Wilkinson, B.H. (1985). Kinetic control of morphology, composition, and mineralogy of abiotic sedimentary carbonates. <i>Journal of Sedimentary Petrology</i> 55, 109–119.","doi":"10.1306/212F862A-2B24-11D7-8648000102C1865D","url":null,"note":"Equant versus acicular habit as a record of reactant supply rate.","tags":["morphology"]}],"cells":[{"id":"Te","row":0,"col":0,"label":"Te","sub":"","title":"Te: tabular (paper spar), c{0001} at 0.3 · terminated by the flat rhombohedron e{011̄8} (nailhead cap)","description":"tabu